# Task Tracer 4.14.0 — static distribution

This archive contains the browser application, deployment headers, icons, translations, third-party notices and an SHA-256 integrity manifest. It does not contain an account service, cloud sync or a payment service.

## Using the workspace

A new workspace starts empty. **Try example tasks** optionally adds three labeled ordinary tasks with reminders off; they can be edited, deleted or undone. Empty workspaces are not repopulated on reload. **Settings & data → Help & about → Help and support** opens actions and shortcuts first, with capture, search, dates, backup, offline and privacy details in expandable sections. Diagnostics are also directly available in **Help & about**, even when storage is blocked.

Use **Add task** in the top bar to open the full form directly. The adjacent **Quick capture** arrow opens text capture with a deadline, project and tag preview; **Full form** carries its text into the full form. Unsubmitted Quick capture text is continued by the main **Add task** action, while `Ctrl/Cmd + I` opens an independent new form. The editor separates title, description and subtasks from project, due date and visible tags; **Reminders & repeat** expands below both columns. The sidebar opens all tasks, your manually chosen **Today’s plan**, projects and saved views. On narrow screens, the navigation button opens the sidebar. Search and project/status filters cover the complete workspace. **Save view** stores the current search, filters and view for reopening from the sidebar.

Saving the task includes text waiting in the subtask field without requiring **+** first. In List, Calendar or Timeline, if the saved task is outside the current filters, calendar day or loaded page, **Open task** in the success message opens it without automatically changing those conditions.

The project field suggests existing names while accepting a new one. **Today**, **Tomorrow** and **Choose** offer direct date choices; the native **No deadline** checkbox remains available. Temporarily disabling date, reminder or repeat fields preserves their draft values; saving normalizes them for the selected schedule. Clearing all selected repeat weekdays blocks saving with an inline request to choose a day, rather than silently restoring a rule. Deleting another subtask preserves the active inline edit’s raw text and selection.

New tasks in the full form and Quick capture inherit a selected project. The tasks-without-a-project scope keeps them unassigned; **All projects** defaults to `Inbox`. An explicit `/project` in Quick capture takes priority. Creating a task while **Today’s plan** is active adds it to that plan without changing its deadline. If the plan has tasks but search or filters hide them, clear those conditions while staying in the plan.

The desktop sidebar keeps the brand and List, Calendar, Timeline and Statistics at the top, with **Settings & data** at the bottom. The middle scroll area contains scopes, every project and saved views. On mobile, views stay in the bottom navigation and opening the drawer brings the active scope into view. Settings has five panels: **Appearance & language**, **Backups & transfer**, **Reminders & installation**, **Task maintenance** and **Help & about**. Settings initially opens **Appearance & language** and retains the chosen category while the page stays open. Returning to tasks keeps filters and unfinished Quick capture text. Maintenance shows the workspace-wide completed, unarchived count; archive/delete controls are disabled at zero.

Open **Settings & data → Appearance & language** for four complete **Workspace theme** previews: **Iris**, **Ocean**, **Pine** and **Berry**. The native radio choices preview the sidebar, canvas, header and content; each theme also supplies its own fields, raised menus, text, controls and selected states in light and dark appearance. Appearance (**Light**, **Dark**, **System**) and interface language (**简体中文**, **English**) remain separate native dropdowns. System follows the operating system’s colour preference. Changing a theme preserves current input, tasks and selection without rewriting user content.

Theme preferences stay in this browser profile. Existing Iris/Ocean/Pine/Berry choices carry forward to the matching workspace themes; task JSON backups still exclude app preferences. Themes are included in the local application, with the same initial-cache and browser limits for offline use described below.

Mark a task complete with its leading completion control. Open its **Task actions** menu for available actions such as editing, adding it to today’s plan, snoozing, skipping a repeat occurrence, archiving or deleting. Actions that do not apply to a task are unavailable. The top-bar Undo action restores the latest eligible task change; concurrent newer work can make an old Undo unavailable. On very narrow screens, header Undo uses its icon and retains an accessible name.

Hovering or focusing a feedback message pauses its remaining display time; leaving resumes the remainder. Its **Undo** action returns focus to an available task control.

Single-task and batch archiving both move unarchived tasks out of everyday lists, including unfinished tasks; their completion state is kept. A new occurrence generated from a repeating task does not inherit the previous occurrence’s manual today-plan selection. Snoozed tasks keep the scheduled reminder time visible, with **Notifications are off** when permission or the app setting prevents notifications. A saved reminder time does not guarantee delivery after the app closes.

On narrow screens, the three task entries each use a readable row. Statistics starts with three task entries: overdue, due today and undated ongoing work. Counts follow the current project and search, independently of the task-status dropdown; zero-count entries are disabled. Open an entry or project row to inspect its tasks, then use **Back to statistics** to restore the source view. Manually changing the task scope ends that return context. The dated seven-day completion bars, completed-today count and streak include retained archived completions in the same scope. They describe saved completion records, not a reconstructed history of deleted tasks; inventory details remain expandable.

The seven-day date index and small month calendar open that date in the full Calendar view. On narrow screens, **Show schedule** opens the calendar from the today-deadline summary. Choosing a day changes what you are viewing, not a task’s deadline. Today’s plan is a manual collection and remains distinct from tasks due today. List, Calendar, Timeline and Statistics continue to use the same task data.

Calendar task actions restore focus to the original agenda task or date after a redraw. Timeline rows show a compact clock and short status within date groups; accessible text and hover details retain the full deadline, while ordinary lists keep full date presentation. Project badges are omitted when the group or project view already supplies that context and remain in cross-project searches.

Useful shortcuts include `Ctrl/Cmd + I` to add a task, `Ctrl/Cmd + K` to focus search, and `Ctrl/Cmd + P` to open the command palette. Task menus support arrow keys, Home, End and Escape; closing a dialog returns focus to an available initiating control. Shortcuts do not take over text input during input-method composition.

## Hosting and upgrading

Deploy the files together on a dedicated HTTPS origin. `vercel.json` supplies script-hash CSP and other response headers on Vercel; configure equivalent response headers on another host. The Vercel config also explicitly skips dependency installation and build commands and serves this static directory; Python and npm are not hosting requirements. For local evaluation, extract the archive and run `python3 -m http.server 8080` from its directory. Opening `index.html` as a file is not a supported PWA installation method.

Tasks and recovery history stay in the current browser profile. Export a backup before changing domains, moving devices, clearing site data, or upgrading. Close older app tabs before reopening the new version. Recovery points do not protect against loss of the browser profile or device. Browser reminders depend on the app/browser lifecycle and are not guaranteed system alarms.

The interface update keeps database version 2, task backup JSON 2.4 and saved-view file 1.1, with saved-view 1.0 import support. Keeping these formats does not make every older interface compatible with newer settings or view scopes; follow the older-window guidance below.

## Tasks, dates and large workspaces

New or changed deadlines use the current device timezone. If a clock change skips the entered wall time, it moves forward by the gap; if the time occurs twice, the first occurrence is used. Check the actual displayed deadline. Editing other fields without changing the date/time preserves the stored instant, including a previously stored second occurrence. Repeated tasks advance their calendar date in the task's stored timezone, independently of the device timezone.

Time-dependent search, today’s plan, overdue filters and statistics are refreshed on minute checks and when the page becomes visible again. Refresh can wait while a dialog, inline edit, text selection, drag or selected batch operation is active. Browsers may suspend timers in the background; this is not an exact-time background service. The overdue rate counts overdue ongoing tasks divided by ongoing tasks with deadlines; completed, archived and undated tasks are excluded from that denominator.

The visible **Select** control beside **Filters** enters batch mode; **More actions** opens a floating menu without changing the current scope or selection. During input-method composition, Enter, Escape and arrow keys remain with text entry. Batch mode starts with 100 task entries and supports Show more. **Select all matching** selects the entire search/project/status scope, including tasks not yet displayed; the selection count shows the operation’s scope. **More actions → Move to project** shows the selected count and accepts an existing or new project name. Moving selected tasks supports Undo. Loading more retains selection, while range changes drop selections outside the new scope. Newer concurrent changes can withhold or invalidate Undo rather than overwrite newer data.

Ordinary and batch lists/timelines start with 100 matching tasks. Calendar undated tasks and selected-day details each start at 100 with independent Show more controls, including batch mode; matching counts and search still cover all tasks. Manual ordering outside batch mode retains full-list rendering costs.

## Backups, imports and recovery

Open **Settings & data → Backups & transfer → Recovery history** to compare the current task count with the selected checkpoint’s count. **Review recovery point tasks** is initially collapsed; expand it to see names/statuses, beginning with 100 rows and full counts. The restore action names the target count and is disabled until the replacement checkbox is selected; explicit confirmation then replaces the current tasks. Export this recovery point downloads importable task JSON 2.4, including empty checkpoints, without updating current-task backup coverage. Clear recovery history requires separate confirmation and removes only local checkpoints; if history changed, review the refreshed list and confirm again. Current tasks, views, settings, downloaded files and Undo remain. Deleted tasks may still have copies in old checkpoints, and later task writes create new checkpoints.

After starting a task JSON download, the app records the offered snapshot’s task revision and export date in this browser; these are local coverage records, not new task JSON fields. A download starting does not prove the file was saved or retained: inspect the actual file and keep an external copy. Changed tasks or older date-only export records require another export before coverage can be considered current. JSON and CSV export each read a consistent task snapshot without refreshing the visible task list.

The import preview uses explicit native **Merge into current tasks** / **Replace all current tasks** radio choices, with replacement selected initially. Merge shows the actual additions, local replacements, skipped file entries, retained current tasks and resulting total; replacement compares all current tasks with the file’s target total. Switching modes or conflict choices updates these counts and the action label. Passed file checks can be expanded. Replacement requires a downloaded pre-import snapshot; merging changes first keeps the current tasks as a local recovery point. Local recovery shares browser storage and does not replace an external backup.

JSON backup restores supported task fields, including subtasks, projects, tags, schedules, reminders and today’s plan. It excludes saved views, app settings and recovery checkpoints. Use **Settings & data → Backups & transfer → Transfer saved views**, under **Other data tools**, for a separate views JSON file (new exports use 1.1; imports also accept 1.0; up to 12 views and 1 MiB). Its import preview shows additions, duplicates and capacity skips; it preserves existing views and never evicts them to make space. If another window changes the views, review the updated preview before confirming again. View files can contain private search terms, project names and tags. Task-file reading can be cancelled, and changes while an import preview is open require a fresh review and safety confirmation. Merge conflict rows load in batches of 100; the selected bulk action applies to all matching names, while changing it resets individual row choices. CSV, also in **Other data tools**, exports all tasks for spreadsheet reports and prefixes formula-like cells with an apostrophe; it cannot be imported for restoration or counted as a backup.

**Browser storage protection** in **Backups & transfer → Other data tools** offers **Review storage status** for usage and persistence information. Its dialog uses the same name; browser persistence still cannot replace an external backup.

## Unsaved work and storage failures

Edited task forms ask whether to continue editing or discard changes before Cancel, close-icon, Escape or backdrop closure. Browser departure warnings are requested for nonblank quick-add text, an unsaved task form or retained draft queue and remain subject to browser rules. Drafts are not automatically written to persistent storage or a server; save or copy important text before leaving. Pending subtask text participates in unsaved-change confirmation and retained-draft recovery.

If storage fails with unsaved quick-add text, that field stays read-only and selectable for copying. Retry reconnects storage in the same page while quick-add text or retained forms remain, keeping the original text and captured source details. After recovery, explicitly submit again; Retry does not replay the add. Quick-add text is only in page memory and is not part of the full-form draft download. Copy it outside the app before refresh or close; a crash can still lose it.

When a storage failure interrupts an edited form, a banner retains it only in this page's memory. Download unsaved drafts exports all retained forms as a separate `task-draft` JSON reference file, not an importable task backup; verify that the file was saved. Retry reconnects storage in the same page while drafts remain. Resume editing opens the next draft after storage is available and respects stale-edit checks; finish the current form first. Discarding the next retained draft requires confirmation. Refreshing, closing or crashing the page loses memory-only drafts. Draft files may contain private unsaved text and the original edited task; review before sharing.

## Saved views and older interfaces

Before changing saved views in 4.7 or newer, close or refresh all older app tabs and installed-app windows: versions before 4.7 can read typed scope objects as All projects and write that interpretation back. Keep a views 1.1 export before downgrading; old apps cannot import it, so return to 4.7 or newer to restore the views. Unchanged task/database formats do not make new view configurations compatible with old interfaces.

Project scopes distinguish real names such as `all` and `__none__` from All projects and tasks without a project. A saved project that is currently absent stays empty. Up to 12 distinct views can be saved. Adding another at capacity keeps existing views and the name draft. Saving matching search/filter/layout conditions opens **Update saved view**, pre-fills the existing name and uses **Update view** to retain the same shortcut.

The saved-view × opens a confirmation naming that view, with Cancel focused by default. Only explicit deletion removes the selected view; tasks remain unchanged. View deletion cannot be undone, so export a views file first if you need to retain it.

## Offline use and diagnostics

Offline startup requires a completed initial app-shell cache. HTML includes its own matching translation data if an external language request fails; this does not replace caching the other required files. Old open windows retain their installed worker policy. Deploy all files together, save drafts before refreshing, and close older windows after upgrading. Clearing site data also removes the offline copy.

Use **Settings & data → Help & about → Check for updates** for an explicit update check and its status. An update must finish preparing before **Refresh** is offered. Finish or resolve unsubmitted capture text, unsaved forms and retained drafts first; saving or pending task changes also block the refresh. When applying a prepared update, the app waits for the worker to take control instead of forcing a reload after a fixed delay. Offline, unsupported and failed checks keep their own status and can be retried when conditions allow.

**Settings & data → Help & about → Review diagnostic information** opens a local JSON preview with a separate download action. The same action is available in **Help and support**. The report contains app/browser versions, capabilities, permission status and storage estimates; it excludes task content, saved views, app settings, URLs and raw error messages. Nothing is uploaded automatically. Diagnostic files are not task backups.

## Integrity and licensing

Verify the archive against its accompanying `.zip.sha256` file before extraction. The builder uses an explicit 23-file source list, plus generated `integrity.json`, rather than including whole resource directories. `integrity.json` lists the exact file hashes, source commit and whether it was built from a modified working tree. These hashes detect accidental changes; they are not a publisher signature or a commercial entitlement.

The included `LICENSE` permits personal non-commercial use. Commercial use requires a separate agreement from the copyright owner, muquew; possession of this archive alone grants no commercial authorization. A buyout or professional edition is planned, but price, payment, private support and issued commercial terms remain undecided. Keep the project license and `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` with redistributed copies; the latter includes the Tabler Icons MIT notice.

Source, development instructions and security reporting: https://github.com/muquew/Task-Tracer
